                <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
                <format attr='margin' value='4' />
              </zone-style>
            </zone>
          </zone>
          <zone h='1600' id='136' type-v2='empty' w='98770' x='615' y='83400'>
            <zone-style>
              <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
              <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
              <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
              <format attr='margin' value='4' />
            </zone-style>
          </zone>
          <zone forceUpdate='true' h='3150' id='140' type-v2='text' w='98770' x='615' y='17300'>
            <formatted-text>
              <run>* An order can have several claim requests, each claim request can have a different AntiFraud Decision. This parameter is introduced to control the level of granularity displayed in the dashboard. By default it is set to &apos;</run>
              <run bold='true'>Yes&apos;</run>
              <run>, which means the dashboard will display data based on the </run>
              <run bold='true'>latest claim request per order</run>
              <run>, reflecting the most recent Anti-Fraud decision. For example if an order has 10 claim requests, selecting &apos;Yes&apos; will count only </run>
              <run bold='true'>one</run>
              <run> (the latest) claim request and its corresponding decision. If you set the parameter to &apos;</run>
              <run bold='true'>No&apos;</run>
              <run>, the dashboard will display </run>
              <run bold='true'>all</run>
              <run> claim requests individually, showing all 10 claims and their respective Anti-Fraud decisions.</run>
            </formatted-text>
            <zone-style>
              <format attr='border-color' value='#000000' />
              <format attr='border-style' value='none' />
              <format attr='border-width' value='0' />
              <format attr='margin' value='4' />
